It's great to hear all the enthusiasm!

@Michael Di Salvo , hopefully Allen, Ken, and Michael will chime in. I'm already in contact with Adam, and he's been helping me beta test. I believe War used a DVD Amaray case. Most of my Videocade boxes are in poor condition after 40+ years. Ideally, the box would look original but be sturdier and open more like a digipak.

@Matt Pilz , I'd also prefer not to cannibalize original Videocades for their shells. In my video, I used a modified version of the Thingiverse shell. I added a socket and moved the EPROM to the top so it can be swapped out without disassembly. I also added M2.5 threaded inserts, making the cases sturdier. My cases look good, but my 8-year-old CR-10 can’t achieve the level of quality I’d strive for in a paid, limited run. The vendor I use for PCBs also offers 3D printing, which is an option, but since they’re overseas, it could impact quality control or cause delays.

@etwelty , thanks for the offer! I'm thinking about using a PLCC-32 EEPROM. I found a good deal on some with high endurance and data retention. I figure I could have the PCB vendor solder the socket and capacitor to reduce assembly time while still allowing for easy reprogramming. They also offer color silk screening for PCBs, so I was thinking of using a white SMT PCB with the Videocade label silkscreened on the back—then modifying the shell so the label remains visible.
